      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
  "correspondence": "Move a split pair to a final coordinate, apply induction on the remaining coordinate summand, then permute the coordinates. Covectors move by λ∘g⁻¹. Restricting and padding identify the common kernel with R^(n−a). The ordinary exactness provider applies at b+s≤n−a. Its positive-s case comes from linear and affine coordinate links; the affine endpoint uses the partner x∈e_j+L_n and the duals λ=h−h(x)δ_j, μ=δ_j−v_jλ. At n=s the bound leaves a singleton prefix and tail length 0, whose augmented boundary is the empty list. For v=(2,4,3), f=(−1,0,1), n=j=2, this gives x=(1,0,1), λ=(−1,0,1), μ=(3,0,−2). Its s=0 case cones in the zero ring.",
      "review": "Checked the action on both entries, row-shortening quantifiers, the two boundary relations, the ordinary-list threshold and the degenerate ring. The choice of simultaneous duals is part of a split generator."
    },
